<body>

<div class="hero">
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?</h1>
  <h2>天空为什么是蓝色的？</h2>
  <p class="tagline">One question, three audiences — a toddler, a high-schooler, and a physics PhD candidate. 一个问题的三种讲法。</p>
</div>

<nav class="themebar">
  <button class="chip" data-target="toddler">🧸 Toddler · 3岁小朋友</button>
  <button class="chip" data-target="teen">🎓 High School · 高中生</button>
  <button class="chip" data-target="phd">📐 PhD · 博士生</button>
  <button class="chip" id="themeToggle">🌗 Theme · 主题</button>
</nav>

<main>

  <!-- ============ TODDLER ============ -->
  <section class="audience" id="toddler">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h3>🧸 For a 3-Year-Old <span class="zh">写给3岁的小朋友</span></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="col">
        <span class="lang">English</span>
        <p>You know how sunlight looks white and plain, but it's actually made of lots of colors all squished together — like a secret rainbow? When the sunlight zooms through the sky, it bumps into tiny, tiny pieces of air, smaller than a speck of dust. The colors bump around in different ways, and the <strong>blue</strong> colors bounce around the most! So all that bouncy blue light ends up everywhere in the sky, filling it up like blue paint. That's why, when you look up on a sunny day, the whole sky is blue — it's all little blue lights bouncing everywhere around you!</p>
        <p>And at sunset, the sunlight takes a longer path through the air, so the blue bounces away and the reds and oranges get to show off. That's why the sky looks pink and orange when the sun is going to bed!</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col" lang="zh">
        <span class="lang">中文</span>
        <p>你知道吗，阳光看起来白白净净的，其实它是好多好多颜色挤在一起变出来的——就像一道藏着的小彩虹！阳光飞过天空的时候，会撞上一粒粒超级超级小的空气，比灰尘还要小呢。各种颜色撞来撞去的方式不一样，而<strong>蓝色</strong>的光弹得最欢！于是这些爱蹦跳的蓝光就飞满了整个天空，像把天空涂上了蓝色颜料。所以晴天你一抬头，整片天空都是蓝色的——全是围着你到处蹦跳的小蓝光！</p>
        <p>到了傍晚，阳光在空气里要走更远的路，蓝光被弹走了，红色和橙色就露出来啦。所以太阳要"睡觉"的时候，天空会变成粉粉的、橙橙的颜色！</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ HIGH SCHOOL ============ -->
  <section class="audience" id="teen">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h3>🎓 For a High School Student <span class="zh">写给高中生</span></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="col">
        <span class="lang">English</span>
        <p>The sky's color comes from <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> in Earth's atmosphere. Sunlight is white, meaning it contains all wavelengths of visible light. When this light enters the atmosphere, it interacts with molecules of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases that are much smaller than the wavelength of the light.</p>
        <p>The key physics: a molecule illuminated by light acts like a tiny induced dipole that re-radiates the light. The amount of light scattered at a particular angle falls off roughly as <strong>1/λ⁴</strong>, where λ is the wavelength. This steep dependence means short wavelengths (blue/violet, ~400–450 nm) are scattered about 4–6 times more strongly than red light (~650 nm).</p>
        <p>So why blue and not violet, even though violet has an even shorter wavelength?</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Solar spectrum</strong>: the Sun emits less violet than blue.</li>
          <li><strong>Atmospheric absorption</strong>: ozone in the upper atmosphere absorbs some UV/violet.</li>
          <li><strong>Human vision</strong>: our eyes are more sensitive to blue, and our brains' color perception leans the scattered light toward blue.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Sunsets</strong> follow from the same effect: when the Sun is low on the horizon, light passes through a much longer column of atmosphere. Most of the blue has been scattered out of the direct beam, leaving the reds and oranges to dominate. A related check: Mars's sky is butterscotch and its sunsets look bluish — same physics, different atmospheric composition (fine dust instead of gas molecules scattering with a 1/λ⁴ law).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col" lang="zh">
        <span class="lang">中文</span>
        <p>天空的颜色来自大气中的<strong>瑞利散射</strong>。阳光是白色的，也就是包含了可见光中所有波长的光。当它进入大气层时，会与氮气、氧气等气体分子相互作用，而这些分子的尺寸远小于光的波长。</p>
        <p>关键物理是这样的：被光照到的分子就像一个小小的感应偶极子，把光重新辐射出去。某个方向上被散射的光的多少大致按 <strong>1/λ⁴</strong> 衰减，其中 λ 是波长。这个陡峭的依赖关系意味着短波长的光（蓝/紫，约400–450 nm）被散射的强度大约是红光（约650 nm）的4–6倍。</p>
        <p>那么问题来了：既然紫光的波长比蓝光还短，为什么天空是蓝色而不是紫色？</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>太阳光谱</strong>：太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少。</li>
          <li><strong>大气吸收</strong>：高层大气中的臭氧会吸收一部分紫外/紫光。</li>
          <li><strong>人眼视觉</strong>：我们的眼睛对蓝色更敏感，而且大脑的色彩感知会把散射光往蓝色方向"偏"。</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>日落</strong>也是同一个效应的结果：当太阳贴近地平线时，光要穿过厚得多的大气层。蓝光大部分被散射出了直射光路，剩下红色和橙色占主导。一个相关的验证：火星的天空是奶油糖色（butterscotch），而火星上的日落看起来偏蓝——物理机制相同，只是大气成分不同（是细粉尘而不是按 1/λ⁴ 规律散射的气体分子）。</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PHD ============ -->
  <section class="audience" id="phd">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h3>📐 For a Physics PhD Candidate <span class="zh">写给物理博士生</span></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="col">
        <span class="lang">English</span>
        <p>The blue color of the sky is a consequence of <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, the elastic (in the Born approximation) scattering of electromagnetic radiation by particles much smaller than the wavelength. The differential scattering cross section for a small dielectric particle of polarizability α is:</p>
        <div class="math">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴ / 4π²) |e′ · (α e)|² &nbsp;&nbsp;(k = 2π/λ)</div>
        <p>For isotropic, randomly oriented particles with a real (non-absorbing) polarizability, integrating over azimuth gives the familiar angular dependence:</p>
        <div class="math">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴α² / 4π²) · (1 + cos²θ) / 2</div>
        <p>Since k ∝ λ⁻¹, the cross section scales as <strong>σ ∝ λ⁻⁴</strong> — this is the origin of the strong blue bias, since scattering of 450 nm light is (~650/450)⁴ ≈ 5× that at 650 nm.</p>
        <p>A few subtleties worth noting:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The polarizability is not constant across the spectrum.</strong> α(ω) is related to the molecular refractivity (via the Lorentz–Lorenz relation) and has dispersive structure near resonance (far in the UV for N₂/O₂). The full wavelength dependence is actually closer to λ⁻⁴·⁵⁶ in some analyses, and the deviation from pure λ⁻⁴ (the "Rayleigh ratio" analysis of the sky) has been used spectroscopically to constrain atmospheric composition.</li>
          <li><strong>The sky's blue ≠ violet</strong> is a joint problem of source spectrum, atmospheric transmission (ozone Hartley band, Rayleigh optical depth τ(λ) = nσ(λ)·L), and human photometry. Under D65-like conditions the spectral irradiance reaching the cornea from the scattered field peaks around ~450–470 nm.</li>
          <li><strong>Mie vs. Rayleigh</strong>: aerosols and droplets with size parameter x = 2πr/λ ≳ 1 scatter via Mie theory, with a much flatter angular dependence and forward-peaked lobes — hence white clouds (multiple Mie scattering) and why haze desaturates the blue.</li>
          <li><strong>Quantum mechanical picture</strong>: the incident field induces a transition to a virtual intermediate state; energy conservation enforces zero energy transfer (elastic), distinguishing Rayleigh from Raman scattering (which appears as weak rotational/vibrational sidebands — the basis of Raman spectroscopy of atmospheric gases).</li>
          <li><strong>Radiative transfer</strong>: the observed radiance is a solution of the transfer equation I(μ) = I₀ e^(−τ/μ) + ∫ ω(μ,μ′) Φ(μ,μ′) I(μ′) dμ′ (single-scattering approximation usually suffices for the blue dome; multiple scattering matters for the diffuse field and the "Barker effect" of the bluer-than-expected anti-solar region).</li>
          <li><strong>Relativistic note</strong>: in a moving frame the scattered spectrum is Doppler-shifted; thermal molecular motion produces a small broadening (~10⁻⁷ relative) that is measurable but irrelevant to the color.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In short: the sky is blue because σ<sub>Rayleigh</sub> ∝ λ⁻⁴ selects the short-wavelength end of the solar spectrum, modulated by ozone absorption and human color matching, and observed through a solution of the atmospheric radiative transfer equation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col" lang="zh">
        <span class="lang">中文</span>
        <p>天空的蓝色是<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的结果，即远小于波长的粒子对电磁辐射的弹性散射（Born近似）。极化率为 α 的小介电粒子的微分散射截面为：</p>
        <div class="math">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴ / 4π²) |e′ · (α e)|² &nbsp;&nbsp;(k = 2π/λ)</div>
        <p>对于各向同性、随机取向且具有实（无吸收）极化率的粒子，对方位角积分后得到熟悉的角分布：</p>
        <div class="math">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴α² / 4π²) · (1 + cos²θ) / 2</div>
        <p>由于 k ∝ λ⁻¹，截面按 <strong>σ ∝ λ⁻⁴</strong> 标度——这正是强烈蓝移偏置的根源，因为 450 nm 光的散射强度约为 650 nm 处的 (~650/450)⁴ ≈ 5 倍。</p>
        <p>几个值得注意的细节：</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>极化率在整个光谱范围内并非常数。</strong> α(ω) 与分子折射率（通过 Lorentz–Lorenz 关系）相关联，并在共振附近具有色散结构（对 N₂/O₂ 而言远在紫外区）。完整的波长依赖在某些分析中更接近 λ⁻⁴·⁵⁶；而偏离纯 λ⁻⁴ 的行为（天空的"瑞利比"分析）已被光谱学用于约束大气成分。</li>
          <li><strong>"天空蓝≠紫"是源光谱、大气透过率（臭氧 Hartley 带、瑞利光学厚度 τ(λ) = nσ(λ)·L）与人眼光度学共同作用的结果。</strong> 在类 D65 条件下，到达角膜的散射场辐照度谱峰值约在 450–470 nm。</li>
          <li><strong>米氏散射 vs. 瑞利散射</strong>：尺寸参数 x = 2πr/λ ≳ 1 的气溶胶和液滴按米氏理论散射，角分布平坦得多且呈前向峰——这就是云是白色的原因（多次米氏散射），也是薄雾会让蓝色不饱和的原因。</li>
          <li><strong>量子力学图像</strong>：入射场诱导到虚中间态的跃迁；能量守恒强制零能量转移（弹性），从而把瑞利散射与拉曼散射区分开来（后者表现为弱的转/振边带——大气气体拉曼光谱学的基础）。</li>
          <li><strong>辐射传输</strong>：观测到的辐亮度是传输方程 I(μ) = I₀ e^(−τ/μ) + ∫ ω(μ,μ′) Φ(μ,μ′) I(μ′) dμ′ 的解（蓝色天穹通常单次散射近似即可；多次散射对漫射场以及反日点区域比预期更蓝的"Barker 效应"很重要）。</li>
          <li><strong>相对论附注</strong>：在运动参考系中散射光谱会发生多普勒频移；分子热运动产生一个小的展宽（相对约 10⁻⁷），可以测量但与颜色无关。</li>
        </ul>
        <p>简而言之：天空是蓝色的，因为 σ<sub>Rayleigh</sub> ∝ λ⁻⁴ 选出了太阳光谱的短波一端，再经臭氧吸收和人眼色匹配的调制，并通过求解大气辐射传输方程被我们观测到。</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer>
  Single-page bilingual presentation · 单页双语展示 · No external resources · 无外部依赖
</footer>

<script>
  // Theme toggle (persisted in-memory only; no external storage used)
  const toggle = document.getElementById('themeToggle');
  const root = document.documentElement;
  toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const dark = root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark';
    root.setAttribute('data-theme', dark ? 'light' : 'dark');
    toggle.textContent = dark ? '🌗 Theme · 主题' : '🌙 Dark · 深色';
  });

  // Nav chips: smooth-scroll to section and flash-highlight
  document.querySelectorAll('.chip[data-target]').forEach(chip => {
    chip.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const target = document.getElementById(chip.dataset.target);
      if (!target) return;
      target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
      document.querySelectorAll('.chip[data-target]').forEach(c => c.classList.remove('active'));
      chip.classList.add('active');
      target.style.transition = 'outline 0.3s ease';
      target.style.outline = '3px solid ' + getComputedStyle(root).getPropertyValue('--accent');
      target.style.outlineOffset = '4px';
      setTimeout(() => { target.style.outline = 'none'; }, 900);
    });
  });

  // Clear the active chip when the user scrolls away manually
  const sections = [...document.querySelectorAll('section.audience')];
  let scrollTicking = false;
  window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
    if (scrollTicking) return;
    scrollTicking = true;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      const mid = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight / 2;
      let inView = null;
      sections.forEach(s => {
        if (s.offsetTop <= mid && mid < s.offsetTop + s.offsetHeight) inView = s;
      });
      document.querySelectorAll('.chip[data-target]').forEach(c =>
        c.classList.toggle('active', inView && c.dataset.target === inView.id));
      scrollTicking = false;
    });
  }, { passive: true });
</script>

</body>
</html>
"}

